context.text:
{
  "body": "Your description names three problems, but they look like one system failing in three places. First, the recurring pattern where you agree on who does something and later disagree about what the agreement meant. My conjecture: the agreements are being made at low resolution. 'Who' gets settled; 'by when', 'how often', and 'what counts as done' get left to assumption, and memory fills the gaps differently for each of you. Second, the changing schedules. Any allocation that was fair when agreed silently decays as shifts move, and staleness feels like unfairness even when nobody broke a promise. Third, the friend who avoids the conversations. My conjecture: this is information about the cost of the conversations, not about the chores. If those talks reliably ended well, he would keep showing up. His absence also means any scheme you and the other friend design over his head will fail the first time it needs his input.

If those conjectures hold, the thing to fix first is not any particular chore but the way agreements get made and refreshed. Concretely: have one conversation about the process itself, not about a current dispute — name the pattern of 'we agreed, then meant different things' and propose changing the format. From then on, write agreements down: task, person, deadline, and what 'done' looks like. A whiteboard or shared note is enough; this removes the whole class of reinterpretation disputes at almost no cost. Put a short, time-boxed check-in on the calendar — fifteen minutes weekly or fortnightly — so schedule changes have a predictable place to land instead of producing surprise resentments. Agree in advance what happens when a chore gets missed: a swap, a trade, a small contribution to a shared pot, so one lapse is an event rather than a verdict on someone's character. And speak to the avoidant friend separately and at low stakes: ask what makes those conversations feel bad, and offer the time-boxed format as the answer rather than pressing him to rejoin the old format.

I would hold all of this loosely. I cannot see whether the real fight is about mismatched standards of clean, about money, or about the friendship itself, and your own part in how the conversations go is invisible from here. The procedural fix only works if the problem is genuinely procedural, and the tests for that are in the commitments.",
  "commitments": "Taking this up involves treating three conjectures as live but unproven: that the reinterpretation disputes are underspecification failures rather than bad faith; that changing schedules make any one-time fair split decay, so a renegotiation rhythm matters more than the initial split; and that the friend's avoidance tracks the felt cost of the conversations. The proposed applications are the written agreement log, the time-boxed check-in, the pre-agreed remedy for missed chores, and the separate low-stakes talk with the avoidant friend. Cheap tests are attached: two to four weeks of written agreements should end the 'that is not what we agreed' disputes if the memory conjecture is right; if they persist in writing, drop that conjecture and redirect toward explicit done-criteria or a renegotiated fairness of the split. If the friend will not attend even a fifteen-minute time-boxed check-in, the procedural reading of his avoidance fails and the problem is likely relational, needing a different kind of conversation entirely. Criticisms I accept in advance: the account rests entirely on one party's report, so the narrator's own contribution to the dynamic goes unexamined; coordination tools do not address standards-mismatch, money, or friendship-level resentment if those are what actually carries the conflict; and the check-in system itself can become one more obligation people shirk. Unresolved: nothing in the exposed material says who is right in any current dispute, and I am not proposing to adjudicate that."
}

